      "text": "First, we divide the factorials by both 20 and 25.\n$$(1+\\tan 20^\\circ)(1+\\tan 25^\\circ)=\\left(\\frac{20}{25}\\cdot\\frac{25}{20}+1\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{25}{20}\\cdot\\frac{20}{25}+1\\right).$$\nSince $25$ is the common multiple of $20$ and $25$, multiplying by and dividing by $25/20$ is the same as multiplying by and dividing by $20/20$.\n$$(1+\\tan 20^\\circ)(1+\\tan 25^\\circ)=\\left(\\frac{20}{25}\\cdot 1+1\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{25}{20}\\cdot 1+1\\right)=\\left(\\frac{20}{25}+1\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{25}{20}+1\\right).$$\nNow, we evaluate the product.\n$$\\left(\\frac{20}{25}+1\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{25}{20}+1\\right)=\\frac{20+25}{25}\\cdot\\frac{25+20}{20}=2\\cdot\\frac{45}{20}=\\frac{45}{10}=\\boxed{\\frac{9}{2}}.$$\nThe answer is: \\frac{9}{2}",
