      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We start by substituting $\\left( 5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)$ for $\\theta$ into the left side of the equation, raising each term to the power of four and multiplying the two fractions.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left(1 + \\tan\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ\\right)\\right)\\left(1 + \\tan\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ\\right)\\right) &= \\tan^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right) + 2\\tan\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right) + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sin^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} + 2\\frac{\\sin\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} + 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - \\cos\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} + 2\\tan\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right) + 1,\n\\end{align*}\nas usual we apply the double-angle formula for cross multiplying.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{\\sin^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} + 2\\frac{\\sin\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} + 1 &= \\frac{\\sin^2\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right) - \\cos^2\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} + 2\\frac{\\sin\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right) + \\cos\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sin^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right) + \\sin\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right)\\cos\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right) + \\cos^2\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right)}{\\cos^2\\left(5\\cdot 2^\\circ \\right)}.\n\\end{align*}\nWe can combine the two $\\sin$ terms in $A$ and $B$ into $\\sin\\left(10\\cdot 4^\\circ \\right)$ by the trigonometric identity: $\\sin\\left(2\\alpha\\right) = 2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha.$\n\\begin{equation*}\n\\left(1 + \\tan\\left(2\\cdot 5^\\circ\\right)\\right)\\left(1 + \\tan\\left(2\\cdot 5^\\circ\\right)\\right) = 1 + \\frac{\\sin\\left(40^\\circ\\right)}{\\cos\\left(2\\cdot 5^\\circ \\right)}.\n\\end{equation*}\nThis simplifies to $\\boxed{4}.$",
